<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aoife MacMurrough</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1145–<a href="/years/1188">1188</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Aoife of Leinster, Eva of Leinster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aoife MacMurrough was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>, king of Leinster. Under the agreement that brought Anglo-Norman help to her father, she married Richard de Clare, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> in 1170, and through her Strongbow claimed the succession to Leinster. Their daughter Isabel married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a>, who inherited the lordship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aoife-macmurrough.jpg" alt="Aoife MacMurrough" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Maclise · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marriage_of_strongbow_and_aoife_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
